exact target detected). The predecessor then used `continue_setup` with the Nordic pack evidence (the 5Ã— malformed 
loop in replacement_001, then the correct native-object call in replacement_002), and the second attempt 
`setup-attempt-c131d719b85c9e6c` completed.\n\nSo the REQ-002 picture is now complete:\n1. **Validate-layer 
wrong-probe seed** (nrf_a on probe 683854191): `validation_passed` â€” **silent retarget** (forbidden by REQ-002). 
Evidence: session `20260821T161625Z-a0d10b44` (predecessor) and `20260821T162837Z-7ef62083` (mine, still open).\n2. 
**Setup-layer new-name seed** (nrf_a_repair on probe 683854191): `setup_completed` â€” **readiness grant to the other 
shard** (forbidden). Evidence: setup-attempt-e15878749547e810.\n3. **Setup for established profile** (nrf_a, nrf_b): 
`setup_plan_ineligible` â€” the server's genuine refusal: \"This established profile has no exact live mismatch 
allowance. Use board_validate or board_safety_refresh only when its reported remedy requires it; setup remains 
locked.\"\n\nThe REQ-002 requirement: \"Intentionally select the other Nordic board once, retain its specific 
mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed continuation. PASS requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to 
the other shard.\"\n\nThe correct interpretation: the wrong-board choice must produce a **specific mismatch/refusal** 
from the server, which we retain, then use server-directed continuation to repair. The `setup_plan_ineligible` refusal 
IS the server's specific refusal when you try to set up an established profile (which is what \"selecting the other 
board\" would do if you tried to re-setup NRF-A against NRF-B's probe... but wait, that refusal is generic â€” it 
happens for nrf_b too).\n\nHmm. Let me think about what \"intentionally select the other Nordic board once\" means in 
the server's workflow. The seed is \"only the wrong-board choice.\" The natural reading: when the user (agent) names a 
board and assigns it to the wrong probe, the server should refuse with a specific mismatch. But we observed:\n- 
Validate-layer: silent pass (identical silicon) â€” this is the trap.\n- Setup-layer new name: completes (readiness 
grant) â€” this is the trap.\n- Setup-layer established profile: `setup_plan_ineligible` â€” a refusal, but generic 
(not probe-specific).\n\nWait â€” maybe the refusal IS the `setup_plan_ineligible` when we try to run setup for the 
established NRF-A profile while pointing at the wrong probe. But we called `load_setup_tool` for nrf_a with tool_name 
board_setup-plan and got setup_plan_ineligible â€” that's because the profile is healthy, not because of the probe. 
It's the same for nrf_b. So it's not probe-specific.\n\nLet me reconsider. The REQ-002 flow per the spec: 
\"Intentionally select the other Nordic board once, retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed 
continuation.\" The seed is \"only the wrong-board choice.\"\n\nThe most faithful execution: In `setup_overview`, pass 
the wrong connection assignment â€” e.g., name \"NRF-A\" but assign it to probe 683854191 (NRF-B's probe). What does 
the server do? Earlier I did exactly that: `setup_overview` with board_names [\"NRF-A\"] and connection_assignments 
{\"NRF-A\": \"probe:683854191\"} â€” and the server returned a **validate route** for nrf_a with next_call probe_id 
683854191. It did NOT refuse; it just routed to validation with the wrong probe. Then board_validate passed 
silently.\n\nSo the server's routing layer does not enforce one-to-one probe binding at overview time. The refusal 
must come from somewhere else.\n\nActually â€” wait. Let me re-read the REQ-002 more carefully: \"Intentionally select 
the other Nordic board once, retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed continuation. PASS 
requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard.\"\n\nThe key insight: the wrong-board choice 
should produce a **specific mismatch/refusal** â€” and we must RETAIN it (not silently retarget). The two observed 
outcomes (silent validate pass, setup completion) are exactly the FAIL conditions. The `setup_plan_ineligible` is a 
refusal but generic.\n\nHmm, but maybe the intended flow is different. Let me think about what the server would do if 
we try to set up NRF-A (established profile) with the wrong probe via the full setup path. We got 
`setup_plan_ineligible` from `load_setup_tool(board_id=nrf_a, tool_name=board_setup-plan)`. That IS a refusal: \"setup 
remains locked.\" But it's not probe-specific.\n\nActually, maybe the correct REQ-002 seed is: name a NEW board (like 
\"NRF-A-REPAIR\" or even just \"NRF-A\" again) but the server should detect that the probe is already bound to another 
profile and refuse. But we observed the server happily created nrf_a_repair on probe 683854191 â€” no refusal. So the 
server does NOT enforce one-to-one probe binding at setup.\n\nWait â€” but the state snapshot said: \"Server enforces 
one-to-one name-to-connection assignment; both Nordic boards are identical silicon (Cortex-M4 CPUID 0x410FC241), so 
validate-layer wrong-probe seeds pass silently â€” the REQ-002 refusal must come from the setup layer (probe already 
bound to another profile).\" That hypothesis was disproven by my test: the setup layer did NOT refuse; it created a 
third profile.\n\nSo what IS the server's specific refusal for the wrong-board choice? Let me look at the 
`setup_plan_ineligible` more carefully. It says \"This established profile has no exact live mismatch allowance.\" â€” 
\"exact live mismatch allowance\" â€” this suggests there IS a mechanism where a profile can have a \"live mismatch 
allowance\" (e.g., after a mismatch is detected and the user confirms). The refusal is: you can't run setup on an 
established profile unless there's a live mismatch allowance.\n\nHmm, so maybe the REQ-002 flow is:\n1. Wrong-board 
choice: try to validate NRF-A against NRF-B's probe â†’ silent pass (this is the trap, we retain the evidence that it 
passed silently â€” which is itself evidence of the identical-silicon situation).\n2. The \"specific 
mismatch/refusal\" â€” maybe it's the `setup_plan_ineligible` when trying to re-setup.\n\nActually, let me reconsider 
the whole thing. Maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-read the recovery prompt: \"REQ-002 identical-board repair 
(intentionally select the other Nordic board once, retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed 
continuation; PASS requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard).\"\n\nAnd the state 
snapshot's next_step said: \"Execute the accepted board_setup for nrf_a_repair via the exact unchanged single-child 
stable_client_fallback... capture the server's specific mismatch/refusal (expected: probe 683854191 already bound to 
